{
  "abstract": "We prove that the fixed-parameter fibration of the exotic-quintuple surface of Dujella, Kazalicki and Petričević has no nondegenerate rational section. Its generic genus-three curve has exactly sixteen rational points over $\\mathbb Q(r)$, all lying in the known degeneracy locus. The argument combines a split Jacobian with complete rational-point classifications of special fibers. We exhibit a rational isogeny $J(C_r)\\sim A_r\\times E_r^2$, prove that both elliptic families have generic rank one by injective specialization and elementary two-isogeny descent, and show that explicit sections remain non-torsion at every smooth rational specialization. This makes specialization of the generic Jacobian injective at $r=2$. We classify every rational point on $C_2$, $C_3$ and $C_6$, whose Jacobian ranks are three, four and three. For each, a complete squareclass sieve leaves eight covering classes forming one rational symmetry orbit. A genus-two quotient of the chosen cover has rank one, certified by exact descent with explicit accounting for the fake Kummer kernel and rational scalars. At five or eleven the cover allows only two infinity residue discs; a small divisor calculation supplies an annihilating differential nonvanishing at both. Each fiber has exactly sixteen rational points, all degenerate. The sixteen generic sections exhaust $C_2(\\mathbb Q)$, so specialization injectivity proves the generic classification. A source-only supplement regenerates the arithmetic and independent audits. The result excludes rational sections of this fibration, not higher-degree multisections, the known elliptic curves on the surface, or rational Diophantine septuples.",
